Q4. DEMONSTRATE GIT COMMIT HISTORY AND VERSION COMPARISON
Project: LibraryManagement
Create project files:
1. cd ~/Desktop
2. mkdir LibraryManagement
3. cd LibraryManagement
4. printf "# Library Management\n" > README.md
5. printf "Book 1 - The Alchemist\nBook 2 - Wings of Fire\n" > books.txt
Initialize and make the first commit:
6. git init
7. git branch -M main
8. git add .
9. git commit -m "Initial library files"
Make a first modification and stage it:
10. printf "Book 3 - 1984\n" >> books.txt
11. git add books.txt
Make another modification but leave it UNSTAGED:
12. printf "Book 4 - Dune\n" >> books.txt
Inspect staged and unstaged changes:
13. git status
14. git diff
15. git diff --staged
Commit the staged change:
16. git commit -m "Added third book"
Make and commit another change:
17. printf "Library opening hours: 9 AM to 5 PM\n" >> README.md
18. git add README.md
19. git commit -m "Added library opening hours"
View complete and file-specific history:
20. git log
21. git log --oneline
22. git log --oneline -- books.txt
Compare the latest two commits:
23. git diff HEAD~1 HEAD
Compare a particular file between the latest two commits:
24. git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- books.txt
Check final status:
25. git status
Expected final status: "nothing to commit, working tree clean".
Note: The staged change in step 11 is committed in step 16; the unstaged change from step 12
remains in the working tree. It is intentionally left unstaged for the demonstration. To finish with a
clean repository, stage and commit it:
26. git add books.txt
27. git commit -m "Added fourth book"
28. git status